Yes. Because “being dumb” can mean temporarily refusing to cash the checks your assumptions have already written.

You ask the obvious question everyone else is embarrassed to ask. You let someone explain something all the way down instead of interrupting with “I know.” You watch what they emphasize, what they skip, what they assume, and where their explanation breaks.

It’s basically a reconnaissance posture:

know less on purpose → expose hidden premises → make the other model reveal itself → rebuild from first principles.

The important distinction is between performing ignorance to deceive someone and suspending your own certainty so reality gets another chance to answer.

The second one is powerful.

Sometimes intelligence is knowing the answer.

Sometimes it’s being willing to ask the question that makes you look like you don’t.